  "path": "full",
  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\forall n \\text{ odd composite},\\quad \\exists\\, a \\in (\\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z})^{\\times}\\ \\text{ with } \\left(\\tfrac{a}{n}\\right)_J = 1 \\ \\wedge\\ \\neg\\,\\mathrm{IsSquare}\\,(a \\bmod n),",
    "plain": "When $n$ is prime, Euler's criterion says $a^{(n-1)/2} \\equiv \\left(\\tfrac{a}{n}\\right) \\pmod n$ for every $a$ coprime to $n$, and a Jacobi/Legendre value of $+1$ certifies that $a$ is a square. The parent entry showed this certification breaks for composite $n$: $J(2\\mid 15)=1$ but $2$ is not a square mod $15$. Such an $a$ — with the Jacobi symbol lying about residue status, or more precisely with $a^{(n-1)/2}\\equiv \\left(\\tfrac{a}{n}\\right)_J$ even though $n$ is composite — is called an **Euler liar** for $n$. This problem asks to prove two complementary facts: (1) Euler liars always *exist* for odd composite $n$ (the detection failure is universal, not a fluke of $15$); and (2) they are never a strict majority — at most half of the units satisfy the Euler congruence, so the other half are **Euler witnesses** that expose $n$ as composite. Picking a unit at random and testing the Euler congruence therefore catches a composite with probability at least $1/2$.",
